\section{Normal approximation}{

The interval uses the first-order normal approximation for a scalar
plug-in estimator described in Remark 3 of Gavrilopoulos, Trevezas,
and Votsi (2026).
Supply a chain whose transition matrix is the plug-in maximum likelihood
estimate from the observed trajectory. The chain must be irreducible.
The initial distribution is treated as fixed, so its estimation uncertainty
is not included. The variance function returns the variance in the
\eqn{\sqrt{n}} limit, giving the interval
\deqn{\widehat{\theta} \mathbin{\pm}
  z_{(1+\mathrm{level})/2}\sqrt{\widehat{\sigma}^2/n}.}
Bounds are not clipped to the parameter space. Intervals at multiple
times are pointwise, not a simultaneous confidence band. Nominal
asymptotic coverage requires a positive limiting variance and the
assumptions of the linked variance function. Zero estimated variance
produces a collapsed interval; this does not establish nominal coverage
at a degenerate boundary. Nonfinite estimates or variances and negative
variances produce an error.
}

